<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - HW10, online version: surface integrals, Stokes' theorem and  -->
<!-- the divergence theorem.  Auto-graded and randomized: each problem draws  -->
<!-- its constants in <setup> and the answer objects are built from them, so  -->
<!-- every student sees the same problem with different numbers.  Every blank -->
<!-- is graded on its own, so partial credit survives.                        -->
<!--                                                                         -->
<!-- Notation follows the text: fluxes are written                           -->
<!--   \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma,                   -->
<!-- circulation is \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}, and the boundary    -->
<!-- curve is called C.                                                       -->
<!--                                                                         -->
<!-- Each <solution> is a bare element whose *children* carry                 -->
<!-- component="hw10-solutions"; the element itself is never dropped and it   -->
<!-- carries include-automatic="no".  See the comment at the top of           -->
<!-- source/review1-practice.ptx for why that matters.                        -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-hw10-online" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Assignment 10</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Sections: <xref ref="section-surface-integrals" text="title"/>,
      <xref ref="section-stokes-theorem" text="title"/>, and
      <xref ref="section-divergence-theorem" text="title"/>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every answer box is graded separately, so a box you fill in correctly
      earns its credit even if another box in the same problem is wrong.
      Press <q>Check Me</q> to submit; you may check as often as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>How to type your answers.</term> Give numbers as decimals to at
      least three places, with no units and no <m>\pi</m>. A negative answer
      needs its minus sign.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-hw10-online">

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw10o-cylinder-flux">
    <title>Flux Through a Piece of a Cylinder</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="rad" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="d" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="m" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-expression>a+d</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-expression>rad+m</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="rsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>rad*rad</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="coef" context="number">
        <de-expression>-d*rad^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the flux
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,
        d\sigma</m>, where
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle x^2,\ <eval obj="a"/>z,\
        -<eval obj="b"/>y \right\rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and <m>S</m> is the portion of the cylinder
        <m>y^2 + z^2 = <eval obj="rsq"/></m> lying between <m>x = 0</m> and
        <m>x = <eval obj="c"/> - z</m>, with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>
        pointing away from the <m>x</m>-axis.
      </p>
      <p>
        Parametrize the cylinder by <m>y = <eval obj="rad"/>\cos\theta</m>,
        <m>z = <eval obj="rad"/>\sin\theta</m>. Writing
        <m>\mathbf{N} = \mathbf{r}_\theta \times \mathbf{r}_x</m> for the
        outward normal, the integrand collapses to
        <m>\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{N} = K\sin\theta\cos\theta</m>. The
        constant <m>K</m> is
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="coef" width="12"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The flux is
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="coef.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(coef.value({})))" max="coef.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(coef.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        With <m>\mathbf{r}(\theta, x) = \left\langle x,\ R\cos\theta,\
        R\sin\theta \right\rangle</m> the outward normal is
        <m>\mathbf{N} = \mathbf{r}_\theta \times \mathbf{r}_x
        = \left\langle 0,\ R\cos\theta,\ R\sin\theta \right\rangle</m>, so
        only the last two components of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> matter. On the
        cylinder <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle x^2,\ aR\sin\theta,\
        -bR\cos\theta \right\rangle</m>, hence
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{N}
        = aR^2\sin\theta\cos\theta - bR^2\cos\theta\sin\theta
        = R^2(a-b)\sin\theta\cos\theta ,
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        so <m>K = R^2(a-b)</m>. Nothing here depends on <m>x</m>, and the
        piece of the cylinder at angle <m>\theta</m> has length
        <m>c - R\sin\theta</m>, so
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = K\int_0^{2\pi} \sin\theta\cos\theta
          \left(c - R\sin\theta\right) d\theta
        = K\left[c\int_0^{2\pi}\sin\theta\cos\theta\, d\theta
          - R\int_0^{2\pi}\sin^2\theta\cos\theta\, d\theta\right] = 0 .
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Both integrals vanish over a full turn, so the flux is <m>0</m> no
        matter what <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>R</m> and <m>c</m> are. Reversing
        the orientation only flips a sign, so that answer is <m>0</m> too.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw10o-stokes-hemisphere">
    <title>A Curl Integral Over a Hemisphere</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="rad" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="val" context="number">
        <de-expression>-2*a*pi*rad^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</m>, where
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle <eval obj="a"/>y,\ -<eval obj="a"/>x,\
        yx^3 \right\rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and <m>S</m> is the portion of the sphere of radius
        <m><eval obj="rad"/></m> with <m>z \ge 0</m>, taken with
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> pointing upward.
      </p>
      <p>
        Answer: <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Curling this field and integrating over the cap is a great deal of
        work for nothing. Stokes' theorem replaces the cap by its boundary
        curve <m>C</m>, the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = R^2</m> in the plane
        <m>z = 0</m>, traversed counterclockwise seen from above to match the
        upward <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>. On <m>C</m> we have <m>z = 0</m>, so
        the third component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> never contributes:
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \oint_C a\,y\, dx - a\,x\, dy .
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        With <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle R\cos t,\ R\sin t,\ 0
        \right\rangle</m> the integrand is
        <m>-aR^2\sin^2 t - aR^2\cos^2 t = -aR^2</m>, a constant, so the
        integral is <m>-2\pi aR^2</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Green's theorem gives the same thing in one step:
        <m>\oint_C a\,y\, dx - a\,x\, dy = \iint_D (-2a)\, dA
        = -2a\pi R^2</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw10o-divergence-box">
    <title>Flux Out of a Box</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="x0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="-3" max="0" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="w" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="z0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="2" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="z1" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="3" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="k" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="x1" context="number">
        <de-expression>x0+w</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="cospiece" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="val" context="number">
        <de-expression>(3/2)*w*(z1^2-z0^2)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the flux
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,
        d\sigma</m>, where
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle \sin(\pi x),\ zy^3,\
        z^2 + <eval obj="k"/>x \right\rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and <m>S</m> is the surface of the box
        <m><eval obj="x0"/> \le x \le <eval obj="x1"/></m>,
        <m>0 \le y \le 1</m>,
        <m><eval obj="z0"/> \le z \le <eval obj="z1"/></m>, with
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> pointing outward.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <m>\pi\cos(\pi x)</m> term of <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m>
        contributes
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="cospiece" width="12"/>
        to the triple integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        The flux is
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="cospiece.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(cospiece.value({})))" max="cospiece.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(cospiece.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        The box is closed, so the divergence theorem turns six surface
        integrals into one triple integral. Here
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = \pi\cos(\pi x) + 3y^2z + 2z .
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        The first term integrates to
        <m>\left[\sin(\pi x)\right]_{x_0}^{x_1} = 0</m>, because both
        endpoints are integers and <m>\sin</m> vanishes at every integer
        multiple of <m>\pi</m>. That is why it contributes nothing at all.
      </p>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        For the rest, with <m>w = x_1 - x_0</m> and
        <m>\int_0^1 3y^2\, dy = 1</m>,
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = w\int_{z_0}^{z_1}\!\!\int_0^1 \left(3y^2z + 2z\right) dy\, dz
        = w\int_{z_0}^{z_1} 3z\, dz
        = \frac{3w}{2}\left(z_1^2 - z_0^2\right).
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw10o-verify-hemisphere">
    <title>Verifying Stokes' Theorem on a Hemisphere</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="rad" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="rsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>rad*rad</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="val" context="number">
        <de-expression>-pi*rad^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify Stokes' theorem for
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle y,\ z,\ x \right\rangle</m> on the
        hemisphere <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = <eval obj="rsq"/></m>,
        <m>y \ge 0</m>, with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> pointing outward.
      </p>
      <p>
        The surface integral
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</m> equals
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The circulation
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> around the
        boundary curve <m>C</m> equals
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        First, <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}
        = \left\langle -1,\ -1,\ -1 \right\rangle</m>, a constant field.
      </p>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        For the surface integral, close the hemisphere with the flat disk
        <m>x^2 + z^2 \le R^2</m> in the plane <m>y = 0</m>. A constant field
        has zero divergence, so the total flux out of that closed surface is
        <m>0</m>: the flux through the hemisphere is minus the flux through
        the disk. The disk's outward normal is <m>-\mathbf{j}</m> and its
        area is <m>\pi R^2</m>, so its flux is
        <m>\left\langle -1, -1, -1 \right\rangle \cdot
        \left(-\mathbf{j}\right)\pi R^2 = \pi R^2</m>, and
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = -\pi R^2 .
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        The boundary curve <m>C</m> is the circle of radius <m>R</m> in the
        plane <m>y = 0</m>. Parametrize it by
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle R\cos t,\ 0,\ -R\sin t
        \right\rangle</m>, the direction that matches the outward
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>. Then
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle 0,\ -R\sin t,\ R\cos t
        \right\rangle</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{r}'(t) = \left\langle -R\sin t,\ 0,\ -R\cos t
        \right\rangle</m>, so
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \int_0^{2\pi} -R^2\cos^2 t\, dt = -\pi R^2 ,
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        which is the same number. Stokes' theorem checks out.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw10o-verify-cone">
    <title>Verifying Stokes' Theorem on a Cone</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="h" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="val" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*a*pi*h^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify Stokes' theorem for
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle -<eval obj="a"/>y,\ <eval obj="a"/>x,\
        -<eval obj="b"/> \right\rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        on the cone <m>z^2 = x^2 + y^2</m>,
        <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="h"/></m>, with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>
        pointing upward.
      </p>
      <p>
        The surface integral
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</m> equals
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The circulation
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> around the
        boundary curve <m>C</m> equals
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Here <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}
        = \left\langle 0,\ 0,\ 2a \right\rangle</m>; the constant third
        component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> curls to nothing.
      </p>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Parametrize the cone by <m>\mathbf{r}(u, \theta)
        = \left\langle u\cos\theta,\ u\sin\theta,\ u \right\rangle</m> with
        <m>0 \le u \le h</m>. Then
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \mathbf{r}_u \times \mathbf{r}_\theta
        = \left\langle -u\cos\theta,\ -u\sin\theta,\ u \right\rangle ,
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        whose third component is positive, so this is already the upward
        orientation. Only that third component meets the curl, giving
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{h} 2au\, du\, d\theta = 2a\pi h^2 .
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        The boundary curve <m>C</m> is the circle of radius <m>h</m> in the
        plane <m>z = h</m>, counterclockwise seen from above:
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle h\cos t,\ h\sin t,\ h
        \right\rangle</m>. Then
        <m>\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{r}'(t)
        = ah^2\sin^2 t + ah^2\cos^2 t = ah^2</m>, so the circulation is
        <m>2a\pi h^2</m> as well.
      </p>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Notice that the answer never sees <m>b</m>, and that it is the same
        as the curl integral over the flat disk capping the cone: Stokes'
        theorem only cares about the boundary.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  </exercises>
</section>
